Output files/folders:
final_augmented_runninglist.csv: Updated running list that merges our running_list and Tableaus additions of variants/hex codes. Will show everything from the running list (source=running_list) and only approved new Tableau variants (source=tableau_approved)
pending_additions.csv: Only the new Tableau variants not already in your running list will be within this file.
qa_disagreements.csv: Does not overwrite running list. This is an FYI only sheet. Shows lineages that tableau and our running list disagree with on hex code designations. (optional; set flag False to disable)
retired folder: The previous “final_augmented_runninglist” will be saved with the date to this folder so that we have access to previous versions of our lineage/hex code assignments.  

Intended Workflow:
1.	Run the python script:
a.	It will compare the running list to the Tableau workbook upload 
b.	Identify new lineages and write them to an output file called pending_additions.csv
c.	Sets a "has_conflict" column so we know which lineages/hex codes need manual review:
i.	If has_conflict = 1, Tableau assigned multiple colors in different places → treat as “needs human call”.
ii.	If has_conflict = 2, ⁠Tableau assigned a hex code to this lineage, but Tableau itself did not assign that lineage consistently. The same lineage appears with more than one hex code inside the Tableau workbook. 
2.	Manually review pending_additions.csv 
a.	For each row of lineages/hex codes you will input in the “approve” column as:
i.	yes → you agree this lineage + hex should be added
ii.	no (or leave blank) → do not add it
iii.	Save your updates to “pending_additions.csv” file (do not change name of file)
3.	Rerun the python script
a.	It will re-read pending_additions.csv
b.	Append rows where the “approve” column == yes (ignores no or blank cells)
c.	Writes the updated final_augmented_runninglist.csv
d.	Pushes the “old” final_augmented_runninglist to the retired folder with an appended date. 

